\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces {\bf  Visual abstract of adaptable left--right swim coordination.} Panels A1--A2 schematize the twisted pairwise excitatory--inhibitory modules (Si3R$\leftrightarrows $Si2L and Si3L$\leftrightarrows $Si2R) embedded within reciprocally inhibitory half-center oscillators (Si3R$\leftrightarrows $Si3L below and Si2R$\leftrightarrows $Si2L above) that enforce bilateral alternation. In A1 the right module (Si3R$\to $Si2L) is active while the left module is suppressed; in A2 the roles are reversed, illustrating how unidirectional excitatory ($\blacktriangledown $) and inhibitory ($\bullet $) synapses, together with electrical coupling (springs), episodically recruit one hemi-network while silencing the other. Panel B shows corresponding model voltage traces (thick colored) and simulated neurotransmitter release probabilities (thin curves): red denotes excitatory Si3$\to $Si2 $\alpha $-synapses, grey reciprocal inhibitory $\alpha $-synapses within the Si3 and within the Si2 pairs, and blue the slow logistic Si2$\to $Si3 inhibition that terminates each burst and sets episode duration. Together these interactions generate robust anti-phase left--right bursting over a wide range of periods, providing a mechanistic explanation for how the same four-cell CPG architecture preserves phase-locked coordination while allowing the cycle frequency to adapt across developmental changes in body size, neuromuscular load, and conduction delays in {\em  \textit  {Dendronotus iris}}; compare with the experimental recordings in Fig.~1 of Ref.~\cite  {sakurai2022bursting}. Panels C1--C2 sketch the corresponding whole-body undulations for consecutive left--right episodes, emphasizing that this compact circuit motif serves as an adaptable building block for coordinated swimming across this range of conditions.}}{5}{figure.2}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:antiphase_pattern}{{2}{5}{{\bf Visual abstract of adaptable left--right swim coordination.} Panels A1--A2 schematize the twisted pairwise excitatory--inhibitory modules (Si3R$\leftrightarrows $Si2L and Si3L$\leftrightarrows $Si2R) embedded within reciprocally inhibitory half-center oscillators (Si3R$\leftrightarrows $Si3L below and Si2R$\leftrightarrows $Si2L above) that enforce bilateral alternation. In A1 the right module (Si3R$\to $Si2L) is active while the left module is suppressed; in A2 the roles are reversed, illustrating how unidirectional excitatory ($\blacktriangledown $) and inhibitory ($\bullet $) synapses, together with electrical coupling (springs), episodically recruit one hemi-network while silencing the other. Panel B shows corresponding model voltage traces (thick colored) and simulated neurotransmitter release probabilities (thin curves): red denotes excitatory Si3$\to $Si2 $\alpha $-synapses, grey reciprocal inhibitory $\alpha $-synapses within the Si3 and within the Si2 pairs, and blue the slow logistic Si2$\to $Si3 inhibition that terminates each burst and sets episode duration. Together these interactions generate robust anti-phase left--right bursting over a wide range of periods, providing a mechanistic explanation for how the same four-cell CPG architecture preserves phase-locked coordination while allowing the cycle frequency to adapt across developmental changes in body size, neuromuscular load, and conduction delays in {\em \textit {Dendronotus iris}}; compare with the experimental recordings in Fig.~1 of Ref.~\cite {sakurai2022bursting}. Panels C1--C2 sketch the corresponding whole-body undulations for consecutive left--right episodes, emphasizing that this compact circuit motif serves as an adaptable building block for coordinated swimming across this range of conditions}{figure.2}{}}
